      "title": "How rebuilding old mosques is bringing Sikhs, Hindus and Muslims together in Punjab",
      "content_html": "<p>Many Muslims saw their places of worship destroyed or abandoned during the sectarian violence of India’s partition in 1947. Now villagers of many faiths are coming together to revive them</p><p>Gajju Khan is smiling as he sits in an unfinished building in the northern Indian state of Punjab in the late afternoon. The 87-year-old is hopeful that before he dies he will hear the <em>azaan</em>, the Muslim call to prayer, echo across his village once again when the mosque is completed.</p><p>The last time he heard it here was when he was eight years old, living with his parents in Tira Maloya, a village in Chandigarh, not far from where he lives now.</p><p>Bibi Rajinder Kaur, a Sikh, donated family land to build a mosque in her village. ‘There is no deed better than giving someone a place to worship,’ she says</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/oct/28/punjab-india-mosques-restored-heal-sectarian-wounds\">Continue reading...</a>",
